WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.500
"The Batman" brings audiences a new, terrifying
version of the Riddler to the screen, while

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.040
also offering new twists on old favorites
like the Penguin and Catwoman.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.470
But which characters should show up for the
sequel?

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.910
Watch to the end of the video to see if your
pick made the list…

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.740
Bruce Wayne's side job as a costumed vigilante
doesn't leave him with much time to run the

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.830
massive empire that is Wayne Enterprises.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.600
Instead, Lucius Fox is often depicted handling
those duties as CEO of the company.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.810
In the Batman mythos, Lucius Fox is one of
a few people that knows Batman's secret identity

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.619
as Bruce Wayne — knowledge he puts to good
use by helping Batman stay equipped with the

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.030
latest and most efficient technology.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:38.440
Many of the gadgets and hardware that Batman
employs in the field are sourced by Lucius

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.449
Fox.

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:43.489
The character received his live-action debut
in Christopher Nolan's trilogy of Batman films,

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:45.949
in which he was portrayed by the legendary
Morgan Freeman.

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:50.089
In "The Batman," it's clear that Bruce has
his operation figured out without Fox, but

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:53.579
he's still rather young and can certainly
use allies in his crusade.

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:57.350
Much of his hardware, while effective, could
use upgrades or replacements.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.029
Introducing Lucius Fox would narratively solve
that issue as Bruce advances in his career

00:01:01.029 --> 00:01:03.149
as the Dark Knight.

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:06.890
Batman has enjoyed an enduring and ever-expanding
mythos over the length of his career since

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.690
making his debut in 1939, with the writers
and illustrators at DC Comics continually

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.310
expanding the character's crew of heroes and
villains.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.350
While the Joker, Riddler, Penguin, and Two-Face
are some of Batman's most popular adversaries,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.189
forging a new path forward for the Dark Knight
might require bringing some prominent yet

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:26.570
previously unused characters into the live-action
arena.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:30.610
The Court of Owls is a more recent addition
to the list, courtesy of writer Scott Snyder

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.909
and artist Greg Capullo.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.720
The shadowy illuminati-like group made its
debut in September of 2011 with the first

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.070
issue of "Batman" to hit the shelves after
DC's "New 52" rebrand was launched.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.189
The Court sways the future of Gotham through
its assassins, the Talons — the most formidable

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:47.060
of which is William Cobb.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.430
Immortal due to the use of a mysterious substance
supplied by the court called electrum, William

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:55.640
tests Batman's mettle and even enlightens
him, revealing a sinister history behind the

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.509
city he's sworn to protect.

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:01.829
At one point, William is even revealed to
be Dick Grayson's great-grandfather.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:04.689
Two-Face is one of Batman's most classic foes.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:08.319
Obsessed with the number two because of his
scarred face and fractured identity, as far

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:12.200
as he's concerned, it's often best to let
the universe decide the answers to life-or-death

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.890
questions — with a simple 50/50 chance at
the flip of a coin.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:20.130
In the comics, cartoons, and in the movies,
he was once Harvey Dent, a district attorney

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.010
and an ally to all that was good and just
in Gotham.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.710
After the mob disfigured him, his psyche shattered.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.400
His dark side took root and became one of
the city's most feared gangsters.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.970
Two-Face has appeared in multiple live-action
films.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.890
He was portrayed by Tommy Lee Jones in "Batman
Forever" with a much campier portrayal of

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.650
a deeply twisted individual.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.010
Pre-Two-Face era Harvey Dent even made an
appearance in 1989's "Batman," played by Billy

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.010
Dee Williams.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:47.250
"The Dark Knight" entwined Dent's destiny
with that of the Joker's in 2008.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.970
His downfall and rebirth as Two-Face were
all part of the Joker's anarchist plot to

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.590
tear the city down.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:57.069
All along, Two-Face has never had the opportunity
to reign supreme as the sole bad guy in his

00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:58.299
own live-action Batman film.

00:02:58.299 --> 00:03:02.129
If Matt Reeves took control of his story,
there's no telling what madness this villain

00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:03.470
could cook up onscreen.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.909
If the powers that be decide to continue the
noir-style theme for the second adventure

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.189
of Pattinson's Batman, Professor Pyg would
have a darkly sinister appeal.

00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:16.220
The character was created by writer Grant
Morrison and officially became a member of

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.739
Batman's rogues gallery in 2009's Batman &amp; Robin
#1.

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.430
Professor Pyg is a mad scientist with schizophrenia
who wears a horrific pig mask.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.940
In his madness, he captures victims who he
mutilates in the name of "perfection."

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.560
They are transformed into brainwashed minions
known as Dollotrons.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.000
While he is a conniving mastermind, he transcends
standard criminality in pursuit of his bloody,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.469
twisted vision of society.

00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:42.620
Professor Pyg has no superpowers, only his
creepy appearance and skill with overpowering

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.819
the mind and forcing his disfigured victims
to do his bidding.

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:50.560
The character's high-functioning Leatherface
vibes may be a bit much for the PG-13 crowd,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.560
though.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.510
Jervis Tetch is yet another mad scientist
— one who recreates himself in the image

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.409
of the Mad Hatter from Alice in Wonderland.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:02.010
He even associates with two rotund thugs known
as Tweedledee and Tweedledum.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.769
Tetch has developed technology that allows
him to control people's minds through manipulation

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:09.740
of brainwaves and a fictional level of neuroscience
we couldn't possibly comprehend.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.349
He often places hats or cards with tiny devices
on the heads of his victims in order to take

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:15.629
control.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:19.690
Mad Hatter is actually a creation of one of
Batman's co-creators, Bill Finger.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.420
He's even made live-action appearances on
the small screen from time to time including

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:28.510
an appearance in the 1960s "Batman" series
and Fox's pre-Batman "Gotham" series.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.980
Despite his lengthy history, he's not one
of the more well-known figures in Batman's

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.570
coterie of supervillains.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.480
He could easily put Pattinson's Batman to
the test, although his sci-fi technology may

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.530
be too fantastical for this grounded take
on the character.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.010
While Robin played a key role in the 1960s
"Batman" series, his quirky attitude and catchphrases

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.750
only served to add to the comedic campiness
of the show.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.550
Joel Schumacher added the sidekick into his
take on the Dark Knight in "Batman Forever,"

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.740
but Schumacher's Batman films doubled down
on the '60s-style camp.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.300
"Holy rusted metal Batman!"

00:04:58.300 --> 00:04:59.300
"Huh?"

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.570
"The ground, it's all metal.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:03.790
It's full of holes, you know?

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:04.790
Hole-y!"

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:05.790
"Oh."

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:08.100
A more grounded take on the character of Robin
and how he'd work with Batman in a more real-world

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.780
setting would be a fascinating narrative to
dig into.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.320
The idea of Batman putting a young kid on
the frontlines against hardened criminals

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.100
is already a difficult pill to swallow.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.310
With the exception of a small reference, Robin
was a character Christopher Nolan basically

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.700
ignored in his gritty Dark Knight trilogy.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.340
Matt Reeves could potentially crack the code
and deliver a Robin that's believable as a

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.470
crimefighter and engaging to watch onscreen.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:33.410
One of the more fantastical characters in
the Batman mythos, Clayface might find it

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.840
hard to make a home in Matt Reeves' more realistic
take on Batman.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.770
However, like Bane in "The Dark Knight Rises,"
he doesn't have to exactly fit the mold of

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.680
his comic book counterpart.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.600
Clayface is a supervillain dreamed up by original
Batman creators Bill Finger and Bob Kane.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.010
There have been multiple versions of the character,
but the original is Basil Karlo, an actor

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:53.540
who prides himself in his craft.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.940
In his most recent origin update, he uses
a chemical makeup that ultimately transforms

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.820
him into Clayface, a giant clay monster that
can shapeshift into anything.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.780
He's a fascinating villain that really gained
notoriety in "Batman: The Animated Series."

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.250
Bringing the character into the live-action
realm would be difficult and require a lot

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:15.170
of special effects, but it'd be a fresh move
for the latest Batman on the big screen.

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.880
Batman has a reputation as a lone wolf, but
he has no shortage of allies.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.520
With Catwoman and Batman going their separate
ways in the final moments of "The Batman,"

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:23.900
there's a void left behind.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.960
Aside from Robin, Batman works closely with
Police Commissioner Jim Gordon in the comics

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.520
— and also Jim's daughter Barbara, who eventually
becomes Batgirl before being infamously shot

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.880
in the spine and paralyzed by the Joker in
"The Killing Joke."

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.890
After her injury, she begins operating as
technical support for Batman under the alias

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:40.890
Oracle.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:45.100
With the DC Comics refresh in 2011, however,
the character received a surgical procedure

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.610
that restored her ability to walk, and she
was Batgirl once again.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:52.950
Batgirl is already getting her own film in
the DCEU with actor Leslie Grace donning the

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.180
cape and cowl.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.240
It's likely, then, that Warner Bros. will
want to avoid mass confusion by also introducing

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.180
Batgirl into the world of "The Batman."

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:04.110
However, that doesn't mean we can't see Barbara
Gordon enter the picture and potentially play

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.170
the role of Oracle for Pattinson's Batman.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:11.160
For most of his existence, Mr. Freeze was
a pretty forgettable Batman villain — that

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.690
is, until a heart-rending episode of "Batman:
The Animated Series" titled "Heart of Ice"

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.100
totally overhauled him into a tragic figure.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.480
Gone was the cheesy obsession with ice that
made him a bit of a joke.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:26.380
Instead, Victor Fries was given a tragic backstory
and a motive for his villainous ambitions:

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.580
His wife was dying.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.850
In an effort to find a cure for her condition,
he cryogenically froze her, but a lab accident

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.090
left him imprisoned in his cold suit.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:38.450
He developed weaponry to match his icy condition
and typically only engaged in criminal behavior

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.960
in an effort to further the research to save
his wife.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.320
Arnold Schwarzenegger played the character
in 1997's "Batman &amp; Robin," but it left much

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:49.320
to be desired, reducing Mr. Freeze to a collection
of puns.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.710
"All right everyone!

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:53.840
Chill.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:54.970
Chill.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.110
Chill!"

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:59.741
The world of Pattinson's Batman could be the
exact setting needed to offer the character

00:07:59.741 --> 00:08:01.700
the emotional depth he deserves.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:06.240
Besides, sympathetic villains are some of
the most interesting characters to follow.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.890
The brutal mercenary and assassin Slade Wilson
is a fan favorite, whose most famous storylines

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.190
involve contracts he takes to kill major superheroes.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.930
Of course, he's tangled with Batman on a number
of occasions.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.620
Video game fans even had the opportunity to
take control of Batman and fight Deathstroke

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.670
in "Batman: Arkham Origins."

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:27.050
In the game, the villainous mob boss Black
Mask puts a bounty on Batman's head and Deathstroke

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.500
aims to collect.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:33.599
He's made a few minor appearances in the DCEU,
played by Joe Manganiello — though who knows

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:35.719
if that version will ever return?

00:08:35.719 --> 00:08:39.560
Making Batman a target in a "The Batman" sequel
could amp up the narrative's intensity as

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.269
the Dark Knight attempts to keep his secret
identity a secret.

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:46.560
A narrative similar to "Arkham Origins" could
be adopted as well, with Deathstroke as one

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.620
of multiple assassins seeking a payday.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:52.300
Whatever the case, Deathstroke would be an
intriguing addition to the world of Pattinson's

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.899
Batman.

00:08:53.899 --> 00:08:57.800
There's more to Bruce Wayne than a lonely
life avenging his deceased parents.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.709
Even Bruce had friends as a child, including
a kid named Tommy Elliott.

00:09:01.709 --> 00:09:06.459
Tommy's father was abusive, and unfortunately,
his mother only enabled his father's behavior.

00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:09.589
Tommy became filled with so much hate for
his parents that he attempted to kill them

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:11.339
by cutting the brakes on their car.

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:15.509
He succeeded in killing his father, but Bruce's
own father saved Tommy's mother.

00:09:15.509 --> 00:09:18.779
Enraged, Tommy grew up resenting Bruce and
his family.

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:23.509
As an adult, he adopted the identity of Hush
and began a secretive campaign against Bruce.

00:09:23.509 --> 00:09:27.610
During the day, he was a successful and brilliant
neurosurgeon as well as a plastic surgeon.

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:31.890
At one point, he replaces his own face with
one that looks just like Bruce Wayne's, so

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.050
he can destroy Bruce's legacy.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.950
As Hush, he covers his face with bandages.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.720
He would make a great addition to the live-action
mythos of the Batman.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.600
After all, the Riddler's documentation of
Gotham's legacy of corruption did highlight

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.720
Edward Elliott, confirming that the Elliott
family is very much involved in this version

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:48.720
of Gotham.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.110
Just as his name would suggest, Killer Croc
has the appearance and likeness of a vicious

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.759
humanoid crocodile.

00:09:54.759 --> 00:09:58.889
Because of the mutation that Waylon Jones
was born with, he's been cast out of society.

00:09:58.889 --> 00:10:02.010
This has driven him to lead a life of hatred
and cruelty toward others.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:06.499
In his own madness, he often comes to blows
with Batman during his criminal endeavors.

00:10:06.499 --> 00:10:10.440
Croc eventually became a well-known gangster,
frequently inflicting his unique brand of

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.769
violence on others.

00:10:11.769 --> 00:10:15.710
Because of his condition and subsequent treatment,
he leans into his perceived monstrosity by

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.579
mauling and sometimes eating his foes.

00:10:18.579 --> 00:10:22.211
While Killer Croc might be a bit of a stretch
for the world Matt Reeves has created, there's

00:10:22.211 --> 00:10:26.170
no telling what sort of new character arc
the tortured Waylon Jones could have on camera.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:30.339
Of course, we've already seen one iteration
of the character in "Suicide Squad."

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:34.699
Killer Croc only acted as muscle and had no
real character depth or growth in that film.

00:10:34.699 --> 00:10:37.380
That could all change with a sequel to "The
Batman."

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:39.540
Maybe Batman could dress up as him!

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.449
"When Batman came nosin' around, he was gettin'
closer…closer!"

00:10:43.449 --> 00:10:44.449
"And?"

00:10:44.449 --> 00:10:48.249
"I threw a rock at him!"

00:10:48.249 --> 00:10:52.421
Ever since 2008's "The Dark Knight," fans
have been itching to see Batman and the Joker

00:10:52.421 --> 00:10:54.440
go toe-to-toe again on the big screen.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.820
Yes, Jared Leto played the Joker in "Suicide
Squad" and "Zack Snyder's Justice League,"

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:02.360
and Joaquin Phoenix landed the role in a solo
film all about the Clown Prince of Crime's

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.040
origins — even winning an Oscar for his
trouble.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.649
But neither one of those Jokers directly squared
off with Batman in a full movie.

00:11:08.649 --> 00:11:12.749
The Joker is perhaps Batman's most popular
foe, and for good reason.

00:11:12.749 --> 00:11:16.000
There are many ways to interpret the character,
and he's often highly unpredictable.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.209
The mystery behind what fuels his madness
naturally creates intrigue.

00:11:20.209 --> 00:11:23.790
Truly establishing the character in the world
of "The Batman," would take guts, as it'll

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.110
unavoidably draw comparisons to Ledger's incredible
"Dark Knight" performance.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:33.170
However, it's time that fans see Batman truly
confront his greatest foe on screen again.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.459
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:38.879
Plus, even more Looper videos about "The Batman"
are coming soon.

00:11:38.879 --> 00:11:46.809
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

